Looking at this problem, I need to determine for which λ > 0 the set I_λ of irrationals whose continued fraction partial quotients satisfy a_n ≤ C·n^λ (for some constant C depending on ρ) has positive Lebesgue measure.

**Answer leak check:** The problem contains no leaked solutions or answer hints. Proceeding.

---

## Proof

Let μ denote the Gauss measure on (0,1), dμ = dx/((1+x) ln 2), the invariant measure for the Gauss map T(x) = {1/x}. Since μ is equivalent to Lebesgue measure (density bounded between 1/(2 ln 2) and 1/ln 2), positive μ-measure ⟺ positive Lebesgue measure.

The partial quotients satisfy a_n(x) = a_1(T^{n-1}x) where a_1(x) = ⌊1/x⌋. By μ-invariance, each a_n has the same marginal:
$$\mu(a_n \ge k) = \mu(a_1 \ge k) = \mu\big((0, 1/k]\big) = \frac{\ln(1+1/k)}{\ln 2} \sim \frac{1}{k \ln 2} \quad (k \to \infty).$$

### Reformulation

The set $I_\lambda = \{\rho : \exists\, C_\rho > 0,\; a_n(\rho) \le C_\rho\, n^\lambda \;\forall n\}$ equals $\{\rho : \sup_n a_n(\rho)/n^\lambda < \infty\}$. Since finitely many exceptions can be absorbed into a larger constant:
$$I_\lambda = \bigcup_{C=1}^{\infty} \{\rho : a_n(\rho) \le C\, n^\lambda \text{ eventually}\} = \bigcup_{C=1}^{\infty} \big(E_n(C)\;\text{i.o.}\big)^c,$$
where $E_n(C) = \{\rho : a_n(\rho) > C\, n^\lambda\}$.

### Case λ > 1: I_λ has full measure

For any fixed C > 0:
$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(E_n(C)) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(a_1 > C n^\lambda) \sim \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{C\, n^\lambda \ln 2} < \infty \quad (\lambda > 1).$$

By the **first Borel–Cantelli lemma** (no independence required), $\mu(E_n(C)\;\text{i.o.}) = 0$, so $\mu((E_n(C)\;\text{i.o.})^c) = 1$ for each C. Taking C = 1: $\mu(I_\lambda) \ge \mu((E_n(1)\;\text{i.o.})^c) = 1$. Thus **I_λ has full Lebesgue measure for λ > 1**.

### Case λ ≤ 1: I_λ has measure zero

We use the **second Borel–Cantelli lemma for exponentially mixing systems**. The Gauss map has exponential decay of correlations for bounded-variation observables (Lasota–Yorke inequality / Doeblin): there exist D > 0, θ ∈ (0,1) such that
$$\left|\int f \cdot g \circ T^n\, d\mu - \left(\int f\, d\mu\right)\!\left(\int g\, d\mu\right)\right| \le D\,\theta^n\, \|f\|_{BV}\, \|g\|_1.$$

For n > m, set $A_m = \{a_1 > C m^\lambda\} = (0,\, 1/(\lfloor C m^\lambda \rfloor + 1)]$. Then $E_m(C) = T^{-(m-1)}(A_m)$, $E_n(C) = T^{-(n-1)}(A_n)$. By μ-invariance:
$$\mu(E_m \cap E_n) = \mu\big(A_m \cap T^{-(n-m)}(A_n)\big).$$

Applying the mixing bound with $f = \mathbf{1}_{A_m}$, $g = \mathbf{1}_{A_n}$: since $A_m$ is a single interval, $\|\mathbf{1}_{A_m}\|_{BV} = 2$, and $\|\mathbf{1}_{A_n}\|_1 = \mu(A_n) \sim 1/(C\, n^\lambda \ln 2)$, we get
$$\big|\mu(E_m \cap E_n) - \mu(E_m)\,\mu(E_n)\big| \le D'\, \frac{\theta^{n-m}}{n^\lambda}$$
for a constant D' independent of m, n (for large n; finitely many small terms are harmless).

Let $S_N = \sum_{n=1}^{N} \mu(E_n(C))$. For λ < 1: $S_N \sim \frac{N^{1-\lambda}}{C(1-\lambda)\ln 2} \to \infty$. For λ = 1: $S_N \sim \frac{\ln N}{C \ln 2} \to \infty$.

**Correlation sum bound.** The off-diagonal sum:
$$\sum_{1 \le m < n \le N} |\text{corr}(m,n)| \le D' \sum_{m < n} \frac{\theta^{n-m}}{n^\lambda} \le D' \sum_{n=1}^{N} \frac{1}{n^\lambda} \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \theta^k \le D'' \sum_{n=1}^{N} \frac{1}{n^\lambda} = O(S_N).$$

By symmetry, $\sum_{m \ne n} |\text{corr}| = O(S_N)$. The diagonal sum $\sum_n |\mu(E_n) - \mu(E_n)^2| \le S_N = O(S_N)$. Total:
$$\sum_{1 \le m,n \le N} \big|\mu(E_m \cap E_n) - \mu(E_m)\,\mu(E_n)\big| = O(S_N) = o(S_N^2),$$
since $S_N \to \infty$.

By the **second Borel–Cantelli lemma for mixing systems** (Sprindzuk; see also Hardege's survey): $\mu(E_n(C)\;\text{i.o.}) = 1$ for every C. Intersecting over C = 1, 2, 3, …:
$$\mu\!\left(\bigcap_{C=1}^{\infty} \{E_n(C)\;\text{i.o.}\}\right) = 1.$$

On this full-measure set, for every C, $a_n > C\, n^\lambda$ infinitely often, so $\sup_n a_n/n^\lambda = \infty$. Therefore $\mu(I_\lambda) = 0$, hence **I_λ has Lebesgue measure 0 for λ ≤ 1**.

### Conclusion

$I_\lambda$ has positive Lebesgue measure **if and only if $\lambda > 1$**.

